          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="edge34" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeColor=#526678;fontSize=19;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="router" target="l3">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <Array as="points">
              <mxPoint x="1200.0" y="839" />
              <mxPoint x="2069.25" y="839" />
            </Array>
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="edge35" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeColor=#526678;fontSize=19;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="l0n5" target="finish">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <Array as="points">
              <mxPoint x="330.75" y="3201" />
              <mxPoint x="1200.0" y="3201" />
            </Array>
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="edge36" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeColor=#526678;fontSize=19;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="l1n5" target="finish">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <Array as="points">
              <mxPoint x="910.25" y="3201" />
              <mxPoint x="1200.0" y="3201" />
            </Array>
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="edge37" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeColor=#526678;fontSize=19;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="l2n5" target="finish">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <Array as="points">
              <mxPoint x="1489.75" y="3201" />
              <mxPoint x="1200.0" y="3201" />
            </Array>
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="edge38" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;endArrow=block;endFill=1;strokeColor=#526678;fontSize=19;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="l3n5" target="finish">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <Array as="points">
              <mxPoint x="2069.25" y="3201" />
              <mxPoint x="1200.0" y="3201" />
            </Array>
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
  <diagram name="Can State or Local Government Act?" id="State-and-Local-Power-Flowchart">
    <mxGraphModel dx="2400" dy="3626" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2400" pageHeight="3626" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="heading" parent="1" vertex="1" value="Can a state / local government do ______?" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=39;fontStyle=1;">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="28" width="2280" height="54" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="subheading" parent="1" vertex="1" value="CHART 3  |  Police power is the starting point. Federal limits are the main inquiry.  |  Joondeph, 2026 ed., Chapters 16-20" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;fontStyle=1;">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="88" width="2280" height="35" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="start" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;START: Identify the actor, legal measure, affected person / property, and requested relief.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;States have general police power; they do not need an Article I enumerated power. Cities and counties also need authority under state law. Federal constitutional limits apply to state / local legislation, executive conduct, and judicial enforcement. Identify government action or an attributable private act (see the final sweep).&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#DAE8FC;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="145" width="2280" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="router" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;1. Run ALL lanes triggered by the facts; passing one is not permission to stop.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;A. Federal collision  |  B. Interstate commerce  |  C. Outsiders / newcomers / sister-state judgments  |  D. Property / existing contracts&lt;br/&gt;A law may survive preemption yet fail the dormant Commerce Clause, or survive commerce review yet fail individual-rights review.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#DDF1EB;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="313" width="2280" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;A. FEDERAL COLLISION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Does state action touch federal government, federal officers, or federal law?&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#DAE8FC;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60.0" y="508" width="541.5" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l0n0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;A1. Direct tax / regulation of federal government?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Yes → generally barred absent a valid congressional waiver.&lt;/b&gt; Identify who legally bears the tax or command; an indirect economic burden is not automatically direct regulation.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Federal contractor / employee?&lt;/b&gt; Do not assume total immunity from ordinary state law.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;McCulloch; Chapter 16.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60.0" y="670" width="541.5" height="258" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l0n1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;A2. Discrimination against federal interests?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Federal government, its workers, or contractors singled out for worse treatment?&lt;/b&gt; Compare similarly situated state / private actors.&lt;br/&gt;Ask whether significant differences justify the distinction and whether Congress consented. Status-based burdens can violate federal immunity even without a direct tax on the United States.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Dawson v. Steager; U.S. v. Washington.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60.0" y="970" width="541.5" height="286" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l0n2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;A3. State prosecution of federal officer?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Was the act federally authorized AND necessary / proper to federal duties?&lt;/b&gt; If so, Supremacy Clause immunity can bar state civil / criminal liability.&lt;br/&gt;Examine scope of duty and reasonableness; federal employment alone is insufficient.&lt;br/&gt;Otherwise assess ordinary liability and any separate defenses.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60.0" y="1298" width="541.5" height="258" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l0n3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;A4. Federal law in the same subject area?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;First confirm a &lt;b&gt;valid&lt;/b&gt; federal enactment / authorized regulation and identify its scope. If its validity is disputed, use your Congress chart / Chart 2.&lt;br/&gt;Read the state rule precisely and infer Congress’s intended allocation of authority. Check express and savings clauses.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;No federal displacement?&lt;/b&gt; Continue through B-D; silence does not immunize state action.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60.0" y="1598" width="541.5" height="286" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l0n4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;A5. Does any preemption route fit?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Express:&lt;/b&gt; state rule falls within enacted preemptive language?&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Field:&lt;/b&gt; Congress occupies the relevant field, leaving no room for supplementation?&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Conflict / impossibility:&lt;/b&gt; cannot comply with both duties?&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Conflict / obstacle:&lt;/b&gt; state rule obstructs federal purposes?&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Yes → state rule displaced to that extent.&lt;/b&gt; A floor is not necessarily a ceiling; federal permission is not always a command.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;PG&amp;amp;E.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60.0" y="1926" width="541.5" height="397" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l0n5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;A6. Preemption or commandeering?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Federal rule governs private actors and displaces conflicting state rules?&lt;/b&gt; Valid preemption may apply.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Federal command makes states enact, retain, or enforce a program?&lt;/b&gt; Anti-commandeering problem; &lt;i&gt;New York; Printz; Murphy&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;br/&gt;A state may repeal its own prohibition and decline enforcement assistance, while federal officers still enforce valid federal law. It cannot nullify federal law or obstruct lawful federal operations.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60.0" y="2365" width="541.5" height="314" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;B. INTERSTATE COMMERCE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Does the measure favor local economic interests or burden interstate trade?&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#DAE8FC;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="639.5" y="508" width="541.5" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l1n0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;B1. Is a dormant-commerce exception available?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Congress unmistakably authorizes this discrimination?&lt;/b&gt; Dormant limitation gives way to that extent.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;State buys / sells its own goods or services?&lt;/b&gt; Market-participant exception may apply only within that market; no downstream regulatory condition.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Traditional public function favored over all private actors equally?&lt;/b&gt; Consider &lt;i&gt;United Haulers&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Still run C and rights checks.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="639.5" y="670" width="541.5" height="314" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l1n1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;B2. Does it discriminate against interstate commerce?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Compare similarly situated in-state and out-of-state economic interests. Examine text, protectionist purpose, and practical operation / effect.&lt;br/&gt;Disparate impact alone is insufficient: losing out-of-state firms or changing which industry profits does not automatically show discrimination.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Philadelphia; Bacchus; Exxon; Clover Leaf.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="639.5" y="1026" width="541.5" height="286" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l1n2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;B3. If B2 YES: rigorous scrutiny&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Discriminatory law is virtually per se invalid.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Can the state prove BOTH:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;1. A legitimate nonprotectionist local purpose; AND&lt;br/&gt;2. No practicable nondiscriminatory alternative?&lt;br/&gt;If either fails → unconstitutional.&lt;br/&gt;Health / safety labels do not end the inquiry; test inspections, origin-neutral charges, and other alternatives.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Philadelphia; Dean Milk; Maine v. Taylor.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FBE4E2;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="639.5" y="1354" width="541.5" height="314" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l1n3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;B4. If B2 NO: undue burden under Pike?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Interstate-commerce burden clearly excessive relative to putative local benefits?&lt;/b&gt; → unconstitutional.&lt;br/&gt;Identify the actual interstate burden, benefits, and supporting facts. This is generally deferential review.&lt;br/&gt;There is no free-standing rule forbidding a state’s sales standard merely because producers elsewhere must adapt.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Pike; Kassel; National Pork Producers.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="639.5" y="1710" width="541.5" height="314" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l1n4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;B5. Direct control of wholly outside conduct?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Distinguish regulation of in-state transactions with out-of-state effects from a command directed at conduct lacking a sufficient connection to the state.&lt;br/&gt;The book treats the latter as an extraterritorial structural limit. State the connection; do not assume every outside effect violates the Constitution.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Book pp. 330-332; nationwide employee-benefits hypo.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="639.5" y="2066" width="541.5" height="286" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l1n5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;B6. A state tax is challenged?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Also check the taxing framework if within the question’s scope: substantial nexus, fair apportionment, nondiscrimination, and fair relation to state services (&lt;i&gt;Complete Auto&lt;/i&gt;).&lt;br/&gt;Focus on the homework’s origin-based surtaxes and genuine cost-based fees. A claim that outsiders cost more needs a fact-based, nonprotectionist fit.&lt;br/&gt;Then run C if an individual nonresident is affected.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="639.5" y="2394" width="541.5" height="286" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;C. OUTSIDERS &amp;amp; UNION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Residency preferences, waiting periods, or refusal to honor another state’s judgment?&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#DAE8FC;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1219.0" y="508" width="541.5" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l2n0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;C1. Visitor or new resident?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Citizen of another state seeking equal treatment?&lt;/b&gt; → Art. IV privileges AND immunities (C2-C3).&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;New bona fide resident treated worse because of duration / former residence?&lt;/b&gt; → Fourteenth Amendment privileges OR immunities / migration (C4).&lt;br/&gt;A corporation can claim dormant-commerce protection but cannot claim Art. IV citizenship rights.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1219.0" y="670" width="541.5" height="286" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l2n1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;C2. Art. IV: protected privilege burdened?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Discrimination against out-of-state citizens concerning a fundamental interstate privilege?&lt;/b&gt; Pursuing a common calling / practicing a profession, owning property, and access to courts can qualify.&lt;br/&gt;Recreational hunting, voting, political office, and many resident-funded benefits differ.&lt;br/&gt;Purely intrastate favoritism is insufficient; identify the outsider burden.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;No protected privilege?&lt;/b&gt; No Art. IV claim; keep other lanes.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1219.0" y="998" width="541.5" height="341" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l2n2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;C3. If YES, sufficient justification?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;State must show a &lt;b&gt;substantial reason&lt;/b&gt; beyond nonresidence itself and a &lt;b&gt;substantial relationship&lt;/b&gt; between discrimination and that reason. Evaluate alternatives and whether outsiders are a peculiar source of the problem.&lt;br/&gt;Local ordinances are covered. &lt;b&gt;No categorical market-participant exception&lt;/b&gt;; local hiring preferences can still fail.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Toomer; Camden.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1219.0" y="1381" width="541.5" height="314" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l2n3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;C4. New-resident penalty?  [Saenz]&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;States generally cannot deny bona fide newcomers the same ordinary resident benefits because they moved recently. Apply strict scrutiny: compelling interest + necessity / narrow fit.&lt;br/&gt;Saving money alone does not sustain unequal welfare benefits.&lt;br/&gt;Distinguish verifying bona fide residence from penalizing migration. Portable benefits (such as subsidized education) may require separate analysis; do not treat every residency rule alike.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1219.0" y="1737" width="541.5" height="341" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l2n4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;C5. Sister-state judgment / law?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Valid final judgment by court with jurisdiction?&lt;/b&gt; Generally must recognize it even if local policy differs; no general public-policy exception and no merits retrial.&lt;br/&gt;Distinguish a genuine jurisdictional defect from alleged legal error.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Only another state’s substantive law?&lt;/b&gt; No automatic duty to apply it; own law may govern with sufficient connections.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;V.L. v. E.L.; Full Faith and Credit.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1219.0" y="2120" width="541.5" height="341" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l2n5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;C6. Result in this lane&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Specify the protected person, the right, the comparator, and the fit between the discrimination and the justification.&lt;br/&gt;Keep Art. IV privileges AND immunities, Fourteenth Amendment privileges OR immunities, and dormant commerce distinct.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#DDF1EB;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1219.0" y="2503" width="541.5" height="231" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;D. PROPERTY &amp;amp; CONTRACTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Seizure, access easement, land-use limit, permit condition, or rewritten contract?&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#DAE8FC;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1798.5" y="508" width="541.5" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l3n0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;D1. Physical appropriation?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Title, occupation, property seized, or right to exclude appropriated?&lt;/b&gt; → generally per se taking; small area, private beneficiary, or intermittent access does not itself defeat the claim.&lt;br/&gt;Consider background access rights, trespasses, and ordinary inspection exceptions.&lt;br/&gt;Owner installing equipment herself differs from granting a third party occupation.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Loretto; Cedar Point; Horne.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1798.5" y="670" width="541.5" height="314" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l3n1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;D2. Use restriction instead?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;All economically beneficial use of land eliminated?&lt;/b&gt; → &lt;i&gt;Lucas&lt;/i&gt; per se taking, unless background property / nuisance principles already forbade that use.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Otherwise → Penn Central:&lt;/b&gt; economic impact, reasonable investment-backed expectations, character of action. Consider parcel as a whole; a large value loss alone is not conclusive.&lt;br/&gt;Temporary development moratoria ordinarily use Penn Central, not an isolated Lucas slice.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1798.5" y="1026" width="541.5" height="341" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l3n2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;D3. Land-use permit condition?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Would the demanded property / payment implicate a taking if directly imposed?&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Yes → essential nexus&lt;/b&gt; to the legitimate interest supporting denial + &lt;b&gt;rough proportionality&lt;/b&gt; to the proposed project’s impacts.&lt;br/&gt;Include qualifying monetary demands and permit denials; legislation is not exempt.&lt;br/&gt;If either fit fails → unconstitutional condition.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Nollan; Dolan; Koontz; Sheetz.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1798.5" y="1409" width="541.5" height="314" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l3n3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;D4. Taking found: public use + compensation?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Public purpose?&lt;/b&gt; Broad deference; a genuine redevelopment plan may transfer property to private parties (&lt;i&gt;Kelo&lt;/i&gt;). Purely private / pretextual taking remains barred.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Just compensation?&lt;/b&gt; Generally fair market value of the property interest taken. Small occupation affects value, not whether a taking occurred.&lt;br/&gt;Repeal does not erase compensation due for a completed period of taking. A taking is not itself forbidden if public use and compensation are supplied.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1798.5" y="1765" width="541.5" height="369" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l3n4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;D5. Existing contract impaired by state LAW?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Was the contract made before the law?&lt;/b&gt; If no, no retroactive Contracts Clause impairment.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Substantial impairment?&lt;/b&gt; Examine bargain, reasonable expectations, and ability to protect rights; consider prior regulation.&lt;br/&gt;Ordinary breach is not automatically legislative impairment.&lt;br/&gt;Clause binds state / local legislation, not Congress. A federal measure may raise other rights issues.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1798.5" y="2176" width="541.5" height="314" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="l3n5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;D6. If substantial impairment, justified?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Private contract:&lt;/b&gt; significant legitimate public purpose + reasonable / appropriate adjustment; substantial deference generally applies.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Government’s own contract:&lt;/b&gt; closer scrutiny of reasonableness and necessity; do not defer to its self-interested savings claim. Examine less drastic alternatives and foreseeability.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;i&gt;Energy Reserves; Sveen; U.S. Trust.&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1798.5" y="2532" width="541.5" height="286" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="rights" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;2. Final sweep: government action, incorporation, retroactivity, and other assigned rights&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Private actor?&lt;/b&gt; Constitution generally requires government action. Ask about government compulsion / significant encouragement, joint action, or an exclusively and traditionally public function. Public funding, licensing, or serving the public alone is insufficient. The Thirteenth Amendment is the principal exception. [Halleck]&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;State / local deprivation?&lt;/b&gt; Fourteenth Amendment incorporates most Bill of Rights protections; identify the specific right (e.g., excessive fines: Timbs). Apply any assigned due process / equal protection / speech rules only when facts trigger them; these charts do not replace a separate rights course.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="2908" width="2280" height="175" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="retro" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;3. Retroactive punishment or legislative punishment without trial?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Ex post facto:&lt;/b&gt; retroactive criminal / punitive law makes prior innocent conduct criminal, aggravates offense / punishment, lowers required evidence, or creates a sufficient risk of greater punishment? → prohibited. Compare law when the conduct occurred with the new rule. An overlapping sentence range or advisory guidelines can still violate the rule. Reviving an already expired criminal limitations period differs from extending one still open. Civil economic retroactivity alone is insufficient. [Peugh; Stogner]&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Bill of attainder:&lt;/b&gt; legislature identifies persons / ascertainable group and imposes punishment without judicial trial? → prohibited. Test punitive purpose / effects and legitimate nonpunitive explanations. Both federal and state legislation are constrained.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="3128" width="2280" height="203" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="finish" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;font-family:Helvetica;font-size:21px;line-height:1.32&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;4. State the result for EACH claim; then check who can sue and what relief is available.&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Do not infer legality from immunity or lack of standing. Use the shared court checklist in the companion guide: Article III + statutory jurisdiction where required → standing / timing / political question → immunity → remedy.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Answer pattern:&lt;/b&gt; state authority → all triggered federal constraints → apply facts and alternatives → conclusion / precise relief. If information is missing, identify the fact and explain both outcomes.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;arcSize=5;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=18;fillColor=#DDF1EB;strokeColor=#526678;fontColor=#17334D;fontSize=21;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="3376" width="2280" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
